<h3>What to expect</h3>
<p>This is a fully asynchronous online course — there are no scheduled meeting times. But that flexibility comes with real responsibility. This course requires roughly the same time commitment as any three-credit-hour class that meets in person: plan on several hours of reading, writing, and engagement each week. Some students find online courses take <em>more</em> time than in-person ones, not less, because the structure is up to you.</p>
<p>The course is organized into modules, and you must complete each module before the next one becomes available. Within each module, the expected order is: read the overview, work through the lecture pages, complete the reading, then participate in the discussion and take the quiz.</p>
<h4>A few habits that will serve you well</h4>
<div>— Log in to the course daily — even briefly — to check announcements and messages</div>
<div>— Read the assigned texts more than once; philosophical writing almost always rewards a second pass</div>
<div>— Take notes as you read and flag moments where an argument seems to break down — those are worth bringing to the discussion</div>
<div>— Give yourself time; don’t wait until the night before a deadline to engage with the material</div>
<div>— Try explaining what you’ve read to someone not in the course — if you can’t, you probably need another pass</div>
<div>— Contact your instructor through the <strong>Canvas Inbox</strong> if you have questions or run into problems</div>
<h4>Technical skills you’ll need</h4>
<p>Before you begin, make sure you’re comfortable doing the following:</p>
<div>— Sending a Canvas message and attaching a file</div>
<div>— Navigating to a web page using a browser</div>
<div>— Downloading, opening, and saving files</div>
<div>— Uploading a completed assignment to Canvas</div>
<div>— Creating and saving a document in Microsoft Word (available free through HCC’s Office 365 account)</div>
<div>— Using a search engine for basic research</div>
